<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What may leave the device (issue #176), for API 31+.

  The access and refresh tokens live unencrypted in
  files/datastore/booksync_prefs.preferences_pb. With no rules at all that file
  was eligible for cloud backup and for Android 12+ device-to-device transfer, so
  a transferred image carried a live 30-day refresh token to another device with
  no login. Plaintext on the device is fine — storage is encrypted at rest — the
  copy that leaves is the problem.

  Deliberately an INCLUDE-list. Naming only what may be copied means anything
  new — a cache, a log, another DataStore — is excluded by default. An
  exclude-list has to be updated every time a file is added and silently leaks
  whatever nobody remembered.

  So this keeps:
    sharedpref — reader and player settings (font size, theme, speed)

  and therefore leaves behind: files/datastore (tokens, server URL, device id),
  files/ebooks and files/audiobooks (also far past the 25 MB backup quota), and
  the diagnostic logs at the filesDir root.

  The `database` domain used to be included here too and is gone (issue #364).
  Room runs SQLite in WAL mode, so the durable state at any instant is spread across
  booksync.db and its -wal sidecar. A backup or transfer that copies the .db
  alone — or copies both without a checkpoint — restores a file that fails
  `PRAGMA journal_mode` with SQLITE_NOTADB, and the platform's default handler
  responds by DELETING it: every unsynced position, the offline `pending_sync`
  queue and the acknowledged-items table gone, silently, on first launch. That is
  the same silent wipe #168 removed `fallbackToDestructiveMigration` to stop.
  Reading positions are server-synced anyway, so what a restore now loses is the
  local cache, which refills on the next sign-in. Excluding the database is one
  line; the alternative is a BackupAgent that checkpoints and switches to
  TRUNCATE journalling before every backup, which is a lot of machinery to make
  a cache survive. `DiagnosticDatabaseErrorHandler` logs any corruption that does
  happen, so the remaining paths are at least visible.

  A restored device mints a new device id, which is correct: it is a different
  device, and position hints are per-device by design (docs/position-sync-contract.md).

  cloud-backup and device-transfer are separate sections on purpose — they are
  independent, and device transfer has no size quota, which is what made this
  exposure live rather than theoretical.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
